+# ECON-02B2d9 — Construction priorities and autonomous colony allocation
+
+**Date:** November 1, 2026
+**Project:** Single Galaxy (original space-colony game)
+**Source baseline:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d8_Multi_Source_Allocations_Source_Tests_2026-11-01.zip`
+**Development environment:** Local Linux C++/SQLite; GitHub is the repository, not the primary development hub.
+
+## Objective and boundary
+
+Allow colony administration to prioritize authorized construction plans, reuse *uncommitted* stock across competing projects, and opt into NPC worker reassignment while retaining the single simulation writer, durable inventory conservation, and individual NPC identities.
+
+**This milestone does not create NPCs, materialize inventory, grant AI authorization, or make inter-colony shipments instantaneous.** The interface remains part of the colony gameplay session. Only the host-controlled `PersistentWorld` may transfer modules through `Simulation` Actions.
+
+## Implemented
+
+1. **Persistent plan priority:** Each `sg_construction_jobs` row now has a bounded priority (`0` backlog, `1` normal, `2` high, `3` critical), defaulting to normal for all old and new plans. Scheduling is deterministic: descending priority, then ascending durable plan ID. Plan priority is authoritative administrative policy, not a measure of stock in the simulation ledger.
+2. **Opt-in autonomous staffing:** The new per-plan `autonomy` flag defaults OFF. When enabled, an active, unfinished project with no assigned NPC may acquire one existing idle, eligible habitat resident or borrow one unladen, otherwise eligible NPC from an autonomous donor plan. An active donor is eligible only if it has strictly lower priority; a paused, completed, cancelled, or blocked donor can also yield an unladen worker when its policy opted into autonomy. Existing aggregate non-construction role reservations, workshop staff and active restock assignments remain exclusions. Neither NPC cargo nor material custody moves with a reassignment.
+3. **Return to lower-priority work:** Workers can be returned to a resumed lower-priority autonomous plan after their higher-priority project completes, without forcing a manual unassignment. Reassignment operates on a durable `sg_construction_workers` identity record and preserves the worker's grid coordinates.
+4. **Reservation preemption:** If an active higher-priority crew needs material, has no unreserved reachable local source, and a lower-priority plan has an uncollected reservation at a reachable source, the scheduler releases only the needed quantity through the **journaled** `ReserveConstructionModules` Action. The higher-priority plan makes its own normal reserve/pickup on a later tick. There is deliberately no raw SQL transfer of physical modules, and no theft of NPC-carried, site-delivered, or already-built materials. Each intermediate tick remains mass-conserving and durable.
+5. **SQLite migration:** On first open of a d8 save, missing `priority` and `autonomy` columns are added without deleting jobs, NPC assignments, or world state. Reopening is idempotent. New databases use the complete checked schema. A dedicated test starts with an actual legacy d8-format construction-job table and verifies migration through restart.
+6. **Colony UI and native policy endpoint:** The existing colony construction panel now offers priority selection and an autonomous-staffing checkbox for the selected plan. The validated HTTP route `POST /factory/api/crew_policy` maps to the native `CREW_POLICY` operation. Owner, ship, and site are derived from the host-controlled session; the relay validates token and origin as before and strictly checks integer priority and Boolean autonomy. `CREW_LIST` includes policy status for each visible plan.
+
+## Local verification
+
+| Check | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| CMake/Ninja full native project build | **Passed** |
+| Native CTest non-live group | **66/66 passed** |
+| Native live/relay/HTTP group excluding two older tests | **26/26 passed** |
+| Two separately isolated older live/relay tests | **2/2 passed** |
+| Total disjoint native CTest selection | **94/94 passed**, across three runs; not one all-tests invocation |
+| `node --test tests/*.js` | **93/93 passed** |
+| Offline Chromium crew and supply controls | **Passed** (mocked browser data, not a conservation test) |
+| Real authenticated HTTP crew test | **Passed** including priority 3 save/restart and forged-token, malformed-priority, and Boolean-as-integer rejection |
+| Dedicated d9 native test | **Passed** for plan preemption, autonomous borrow/return, idle assignment, legacy schema, carried-cargo exclusion, restart and conservation |
+| Patch applied against exact d8 archive | Verified by source-content comparison during packaging |
+
+The 28-test live group was originally interrupted by the execution time limit with 26 tests reported passed; both unfinished tests passed in their own run. Subsequently the suite was rerun as a 26-test group plus the independent 2-test group, all passing.
+
+## Test significance and limitations
+
+- **What is proven:** A lower-priority plan's exclusive, unpicked depot reservation can be safely released and appropriated by the higher-priority scheduler with no duplication. A worker cannot be reassigned while carrying physical modules; the cargo remains in the canonical world and survives SQLite restart. When cargo is returned via an authoritative Action, the resident may be assigned to the higher-priority job. After high-priority completion, a lower-priority autonomous job may reclaim the resident. Idleness is not treated as consent unless the *target* project explicitly opted into autonomous staffing.
+- **What is not proven:** Global factory prioritization across all colonies; preempting cross-site transport contracts; predictive material procurement; fairness guarantees if critical jobs are permanently backlogged; congestion, vehicle transport or true 3D NPC navigation. Reservations held at an inaccessible or demolished storage node still require a separate consistency/recovery mechanism; no remote movement of inventory was introduced.
+- **Policy intentionally narrow:** Priority preemption only operates when stock would otherwise block a higher-priority crew. Already staged NPC or cell stock is never moved by SQL. Multiple autonomous workers per project, strategic population management and independent colony leadership are not part of this gate.
+- **Windows:** Native Windows installer and GUI acceptance remain open. This milestone was compiled/tested on Linux.
+
+## Changed source files
+
+- `src/NpcConstructionCrews.cpp`, `include/galaxy/NpcConstructionCrews.hpp`: durable job priority and autonomy, migration, deterministic scheduling and physical reservation reallocation.
+- `src/factory_colony_protocol.hpp`, `live/interactive_relay.py`: typed, session-bound `CREW_POLICY` operation and policy state on crew listings.
+- `viewer/interactive.html`, `viewer/structures.js`: policy selection inside colony construction UI, no separate competing world writer.
+- `tests/ECON02B2d9PriorityAllocatorTests.cpp`: new host-level conservation, worker/cargo and persistence coverage.
+- `tests/ECON02B2d6ConstructionCrewHostTests.py`, `scripts/colony_crew_browser_smoke.py`: authenticated HTTP / Chromium controls regression tests.
+- `CMakeLists.txt`: new native CTest target.
+
+## Recommended next gate — ECON-02B2d10
+
+Implement safe **depleted / unavailable source recovery and material-demand planning**: detect reservations stranded at no-longer-serviceable nodes, validate physically reachable alternative sources, and surface blocked dependencies before a job is repeatedly retried. Do not auto-refund delivered cargo, bypass non-resident job restrictions, or add arbitrary factory-floor size caps. Address congestion and player-away colony scheduling in later bounded, deterministic simulation tiers.
